  "_meta": {
    "managed_by": "dxdfir_stack",
    "source": "elastic/templates/component/logs-dxdfir@zeek.json",
    "description": "The zeek lane model, composed ONLY into the zeek stream (logs-dxdfir-zeek index template) — so its bare per-protocol field names never reach the winlog/log2timeline/detections streams and collide with their ECS/Suricata objects. The connection id tuple is pinned (orig_h/resp_h as ip, orig_p/resp_p as integer) so its type never depends on which log lands first, uid keyword, ts date, and zeek.file_id / zeek.log_source where the native source and file id move to avoid colliding with the ECS source and file objects. opcode is pinned keyword because DNS emits it numeric while LDAP/IRC emit it as a string and every Zeek protocol shares the zeek stream (a long mapping breaks LDAP's opcode:bind* prefix query and would reject string-opcode records to the failure store). The flat keyword/long/boolean fields below are the native Zeek per-protocol columns the Malcolm dashboards aggregate on (NTLM hostname/username, FTP user, SMTP tls/mailfrom, ...): pre-mapped so a panel for a protocol not yet in the captures shows 'No results' instead of a red field-not-found error, and fills in when that traffic is ingested. Within the zeek stream two names still clash across logs and are moved in the pipeline: cmd (a {name,key,value} record in redis, a string in mysql) to zeek.redis_cmd / zeek.mysql_cmd, and a Host-header host string to url.domain rather than the ECS host object (following cisagov/Malcolm, which namespaces every Zeek field under zeek.<protocol>). Suricata's stats/metadata moved to logs-dxdfir@detections."
  }
